detail.abstract:
Background: Chagas disease remains a significant public health challenge in Argentina, with a historically large infected population and continued propagation through vertical transmission. Despite a robust legal framework established by National Law 26.281, the disease persists into chronic stages due to systemic barriers within a fragmented healthcare model.&nbsp; 
<div>
 Methods: We conducted a narrative review of peer-reviewed literature and official national surveillance data, including the Boletín Epidemiológico Nacional N°743 (Ministerio de Salud de la Nación, 2025) for the period 2019-2024, to characterize the maternal-infant care cascade for congenital Chagas disease in Argentina and its economic implications.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Results: Analysis reveals a critical implementation gap in the maternal-infant care cascade: the national "loss to follow-up" (pérdida de seguimiento) rate, a surveillance-defined measure of cases without a documented diagnostic outcome rather than a direct measure of patients lost to clinical care, reached 78% for at-risk infants in 2023, with regional peaks of 96% (Northeast/NEA) and 94% (Cuyo). Of 8,987 suspected congenital cases notified nationally between 2019 and 2024, only 387 (4.3%) reached confirmed status. These findings, drawn directly from the official national epidemiological bulletin and corroborated by independent clinical research, indicate that gaps in continuity of care and surveillance completion have become a major, and likely increasingly important, apparent constraint on Chagas elimination in Argentina, alongside continued vectorial risk in historically endemic areas.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Conclusions: Closing Argentina's remaining Chagas gap requires building on the country's historical emphasis on vector control toward a more fully integrated elimination strategy, one that maintains vector surveillance while substantially strengthening congenital prevention, diagnosis, treatment, and longitudinal follow-up; recent institutional developments, including the 2022 Programa Nacional de Chagas and the emerging SeVICha strategy (launched 2025), represent concrete, if still preliminary, steps in this direction.
</div>
